WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:06.357
The year is 1933, and the world around us
is changing, for better and for worse.

00:00:06.357 --> 00:00:08.019
King Kong amazes movie audiences.

00:00:08.019 --> 00:00:09.874
The 21st Amendment ends Prohibition.

00:00:09.874 --> 00:00:13.680
And around the corner looms a dark period
for the world.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.970
Amidst this backdrop, scientists continue
to fill the world with wonders, conceiving

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.612
of astronomical marvels and medical miracles.

00:00:19.612 --> 00:00:25.275
And as they dive deeper into the invisible
world around us, a rare ciliate makes an appearance….

00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:29.423
And after an absence of almost 90 years, we’ve
found it again.

00:00:42.892 --> 00:00:46.730
We aren’t in the business of news here at
Journey to the Microcosmos.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:51.842
But we do sometimes find something that feels
like it should be breaking news.

00:00:51.842 --> 00:00:56.480
Like when James, our master of microscopes,
manages to find a microbe that hasn’t been

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.000
spotted since the 1930s—

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.159
that always gets the rest of us on the Journey
to the Microcosmos team excited because we

00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:08.162
know we’re about to see something weird.

00:01:08.162 --> 00:01:11.720
And ‘weird’ is exactly what James brought us.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:17.712
The organism that he found is that oval-ish
character with a twisted tail at one end.

00:01:17.712 --> 00:01:23.395
And that tail ended up making it really difficult
for James to get other scientists to believe

00:01:23.395 --> 00:01:25.611
what he had found.

00:01:25.611 --> 00:01:29.990
But before we get there, let’s start with
how James managed to find this microbe to

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:33.553
begin with, which really leads us 
to a question that you might not think

00:01:33.553 --> 00:01:35.376
about when you’re walking around:

00:01:35.376 --> 00:01:38.444
how old is that pond?

00:01:40.632 --> 00:01:44.252
Now obviously the answer depends  
on which pond you’re talking about.

00:01:44.252 --> 00:01:48.610
If you live in a city like James does, the
ponds you stumble across are much more likely

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:51.329
to be on the younger side.

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:57.302
And that poses a bit of a conundrum for James
in his hunt for his particular microbial specialty:

00:01:57.302 --> 00:01:58.392
ciliates.

00:01:58.392 --> 00:02:05.283
Because when it comes to finding hotspots
of ciliate diversity, older ponds are much better.

00:02:05.283 --> 00:02:10.780
Ciliates can spread to new locations as cysts,
which get carried by the wind or animals from

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.240
one spot to another.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.430
So a pond that has been around for a while
will probably have welcomed many new residents

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:22.646
over the years, building a diverse microscopic
neighborhood in the process.

00:02:22.646 --> 00:02:28.280
There is this one particular lake in Warsaw,
though, that's been a curiously rich spot

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.061
for ciliates, which was surprising to James
given how young ponds usually are in cities.

00:02:34.061 --> 00:02:39.450
But when he looked through older maps of Warsaw,
he found that this pond had been around for

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:45.879
at least 130 years, and probably even longer,
giving it plenty of time to accumulate more

00:02:45.879 --> 00:02:50.232
and more ciliates, including rare ones.

00:02:50.232 --> 00:02:55.129
When James found this organism four years
ago, he was so excited to have uncovered what

00:02:55.129 --> 00:02:58.982
he was sure was a member of the genus Bryophyllum.

00:02:58.982 --> 00:03:04.746
He’d found so many of them in this pond, and he 
wanted to be able to learn more from an expert.

00:03:04.746 --> 00:03:09.230
So he reached out to someone who works on
this genus, sending an email that included

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:15.149
images of what he had found and mentioned
just how many of them he’d found.

00:03:15.149 --> 00:03:21.090
But when he heard back, the expert simply
told him that the ciliate he’d found was damaged—

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:26.549
that the weird tail wasn’t actually a tail, it was
just something wrong with the sample he collected.

00:03:26.549 --> 00:03:31.659
And that response was discouraging, especially
given how many other individuals like this

00:03:31.659 --> 00:03:33.290
James had found.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:37.834
But our Master of Microscopes
is nothing if not persistent,

00:03:37.834 --> 00:03:39.819
so he returned to the pond.

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:44.709
And on his way, he posted about his hunt for
a rare ciliate on Instagram.

00:03:44.709 --> 00:03:50.049
And then he got a response from the ciliate expert
Professor Genoveva Esteban, who has since

00:03:50.049 --> 00:03:52.080
become one of James’ main collaborators.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.563
Though at the time, they were just acquaintances.

00:03:55.563 --> 00:04:01.010
Professor Esteban asked James what he was
on the hunt for, and he sent her the picture

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:05.385
of the tailed ciliate, it blew her mind.

00:04:05.385 --> 00:04:11.450
She had never seen anything like it in her
long years of exploring the microcosmos, but

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:19.250
she was able to uncover a German paper from
1933 by a Professor J. V. Gelei.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:25.133
And when James looked through the illustrations
in the paper, he found his tailed ciliate.

00:04:25.133 --> 00:04:27.410
It wasn’t damaged at all—

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:33.250
it was just so rare that the last report of
one went back decades.

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:38.440
It was tricky, but James was able to identify
the species as Bryophyllum caudatum by looking

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:43.410
at specific patterns where the cilia roots
into the plasma membrane.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:48.190
These patterns are really hard to see with
a light microscope, James was just able

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:51.980
to make out those identifying details with
his.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:56.225
It was frustrating though to try and convince
others of what he’d found.

00:04:56.225 --> 00:05:01.449
Even though he kept his samples in a large
tank and kept turning up more tailed Bryophyllum,

00:05:01.449 --> 00:05:06.509
one of the scientists he talked to insisted
that there was no actual tail.

00:05:06.509 --> 00:05:10.830
That what he was seeing was maybe some
kind of remnant of how he pipetted his samples

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:11.830
onto the slide—

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:17.650
that James was stretching and deforming the
cell into forming a tail-like structure.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:23.740
James just rolled his eyes and made a handheld
microscope to watch the Bryophyllum in action

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.285
in the tank.

00:05:25.285 --> 00:05:31.139
And there he could see several of them swimming
around on the sediment, their tails very much

00:05:31.139 --> 00:05:33.880
present without James’ intervention.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.900
And that means the Bryophyllum he 
found were not damaged—

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:45.752
they were just a perfect combination of
weird and rare that does make them hard to believe.

00:05:45.752 --> 00:05:49.400
Tails are not a common trait among ciliates.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:54.280
And even more strangely, James found that
the length of the tail seemed to vary from

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:56.313
individual to individual.

00:05:56.313 --> 00:06:02.229
Given how rare Bryophyllum are, it’s hard to 
establish exactly what the purpose of their tail is.

00:06:02.229 --> 00:06:04.891
But James has a few ideas.

00:06:04.891 --> 00:06:08.229
One is that the tail might be 
involved in helping the organism

00:06:08.229 --> 00:06:13.742
survive in low-oxygen areas, increasing the
Bryophyllum’s surface area so that they

00:06:13.742 --> 00:06:15.960
can take in more oxygen.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:22.138
So individuals that live in areas with lower
oxygen concentrations would have a longer tail.

00:06:22.138 --> 00:06:27.360
But the tail might also help Bryophyllum eat,
because it’s lined with extrusomes, which

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.500
are organelles that can be fired off to paralyze
food.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:37.248
In this case, we can see the Bryophyllum paralyzing
rotifers and turning them into food.

00:06:37.248 --> 00:06:42.428
And here is a well-fed Bryophyllum, stuffed
with rotifers.

00:06:47.151 --> 00:06:51.740
When James was telling us about his experience
with the Bryophyllum caudatum, the thing that

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:57.740
stood out to him most was how challenging
it was to convince people of its existence,

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:02.410
and how formative Professor Esteban’s belief
in what he’d found was.

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:04.389
She didn’t discourage him.

00:07:04.389 --> 00:07:11.076
Instead she hunted, digging through old texts
with the same persistence that James shows

00:07:11.076 --> 00:07:13.374
with old city ponds.

00:07:13.374 --> 00:07:19.797
Unfortunately, despite years of encounters,
the Bryophyllum seem to have disappeared from

00:07:19.797 --> 00:07:21.000
the pond.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.919
But who knows, maybe they’ll turn up 
there again, or in some distant pond.

00:07:25.919 --> 00:07:31.449
And when someone finds it and isn’t sure
of what they found, we hope they can at least

00:07:31.449 --> 00:07:33.126
find this video—

00:07:33.126 --> 00:07:36.860
a letter from a past microscopist who believes
in them.

00:07:39.397 --> 00:07:45.780
Thank you for coming on this journey with us 
as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

